to tune the level of multithreading.\nL269@P2: Finally, Section 3.3 describes Cache-Conscious Wavefront Schedul\u0002ing (CCWS), an adaptive hardware scheduler that uses fine-grained\nL270@P2: memory system feedback to capture intra-wavefront locality.\nL271@P2: 3.1. A Code Example\nL272@P2: Consider the inner loop of a graph processing workload presented in\nL273@P2: Example 1. The problem has been partitioned by having each scalar\nL274@P2: thread operate on all the edges of a single node. The adjoining edges\nL275@P2: of each node are stored sequentially in memory. This type of storage\nL307@P2: to maximize performance. This observation motivates the introduc\u0002tion of static wavefront limiting (SWL) which allows a high-level\nL308@P2: programmer to specify a limit on the number of wavefronts actively\nL309@P2: scheduled per compute unit at kernel launch.\nL310@P2: 3.2. Static Wavefront Limiting (SWL)\nL311@P2: Figure 3 shows the effect limiting the number of wavefronts actively\nL312@P2: scheduled on a compute unit has on cache performance and sys\u0002tem throughput. Current programming API’s such as CUDA and\nL324@P2: In benchmarks that make use of work group level synchronization,\nL325@P2: SWL limits the number of wavefronts running until a barrier, allows\nL326@P2: the rest of the work-group to reach the barrier, then continues with\nL327@P2: the same multithreading constraints.\nL328@P2: In Section 5 we demonstrate that the optimal number of wave\u0002fronts is different for different benchmarks. Moreover, we find this\nL329@P2: number changes in each benchmark when its input data is changed.\nL330@P2: This dependence on benchmark and input data makes an adaptive\nL331@P2: CCWS system desirable.\nL332@P2: 3.3. Cache-Conscious Wavefront Scheduling (CCWS)\nL333@P2: This subsection first defines the goal and high level implementation\nL334@P2: of CCWS in Section 3.3.1. Next, Section 3.3.2 details how CCWS\nL335@P2: is applied to the baseline scheduling logic. Section 3.3.3 explains\nL336@P2: the lost intra-wavefront locality detector (LLD), followed by Sec\u0002tion 3.3.4 which explains how our locality scoring system makes\nL337@P2: use of LLD information to determine which wavefronts can issue.\nL338@P2: Finally, Section 3.3.5 describes the locality score value assigned to\nL339@P2: a wavefront when lost locality is detected.\nL340@P2: 3.3.1. High-Level Description The goal of CCWS is to dynamically\nL341@P2: determine the number of wavefronts allowed to access the mem\u0002ory system and which wavefronts those should be. At a high level,\nL342@P2: CCWS is a wavefront scheduler that reacts to access level feedback\nL343@P2: ( 4 in Figure 5) from the L1D cache and a victim tag array (VTA)\nL344@P2: at the memory stage. CCWS uses a dynamic locality scoring system\nL345@P2: to make scheduling decisions.\nL346@P2: The intuition behind why our scoring system works can be ex\u0002plained by Figure 6. At a high level, each wavefront is given a score\nL347@P2: based on how much intra-wavefront locality it has lost. These scores\nL348@P2: change over time. Wavefronts with the largest scores fall to the bot\u0002tom of a sorted stack (for example, W2 at T1), pushing wavefronts\nL349@P2: with smaller scores above a cutoff (W3 at T1) which prevents them\nL350@P2: from accessing the L1D. In effect, the locality scoring system re\u0002duces the number of accesses between data re-references from the\nL351@P2-3: 3Memory Unit\nL352@P3: Wavefront Issue Arbiter\nL353@P3: Fetch/\nL354@P3: Decode\nL355@P3: WIA\nL356@P3: Waves\nL357@P3: Ready\nL358@P3: [1:N]\nL359@P3: LSS\nL360@P3: LLD\nL361@P3: W1 W2 WN\nL362@P3: Tag\nL363@P3: Tag\nL364@P3: Tag\nL365@P3: Tag\nL366@P3: Tag\nL367@P3: Tag\nL368@P3: Tag\nL369@P3: Tag\nL370@P3: WID\nL371@P3: WID\nL372@P3: Data\nL373@P3: Data\nL374@P3: VTAHit\nL375@P3: (WID)\nL376@P3: L1D\nL377@P3: Cache\nL378@P3: Victim Tag\nL379@P3: Array\nL380@P3: To Exec\nL381@P3: Inst.\nL382@P3: (WID)\nL383@P3: Intersec