0)\nL493@P3: W2\nL494@P3: Finish\nL495@P3: ...\nL496@P3: Figure 6: Locality scoring system operation example. LLS=lost\u0002locality score, LLDS=lost-locality detected score\nL497@P3: same wavefront by removing the accesses of other wavefronts. The\nL498@P3: following subsections describe CCWS in more detail.\nL499@P3: 3.3.2. Effect on Baseline Issue Logic Figure 5 shows the modifica\u0002tions to the baseline wavefront issue arbiter ( 1 ) and memory unit\nL500@P3: ( 5 ) required for CCWS. CCWS is implemented as an extension to\nL501@P3: the system’s baseline wavefront prioritization logic ( 6 ). This prior\u0002itization could be done in a greedy, round-robin or two level manner.\nL502@P3: CCWS operates by preventing loads that are predicted to interfere\nL503@P3: with intra-wavefront locality from issuing through a \"Can Issue\" bit\nL504@P3: vector ( 7 ) output by the locality scoring system ( 8 ). The intersec\u0002tion logic block ( 9 ) selects the highest priority ready wavefront that\nL505@P3: has issue permission.\nL506@P3: 3.3.3. Lost Intra-Wavefront Locality Detector (LLD) To evaluate\nL507@P3: which wavefronts are losing intra-wavefront locality, we introduce\nL508@P3: the LLD unit ( 10 ) which uses a victim tag array (VTA) ( 11 ). The\nL509@P3: VTA is a highly modified variation of a victim cache [23]. The sets\nL510@P3: of the VTA are sub-divided among the all the wavefront contexts\nL511@P3: supported on this core. This gives each wavefront its own small\nL512@P3: VTA ( 12 ). The VTA only stores cache tags and does not store line\nL513@P3: data. When a miss occurs and a line is reserved in the L1D cache, the\nL514@P3: wavefront ID (WID) of the wavefront reserving that line is written\nL515@P3: in addition to the tag ( 13 ). When that line is evicted from the cache,\nL516@P3: its tag information is written to that wavefront’s portion of the VTA.\nL517@P3: Whenever there is a miss in the L1D cache, the VTA is probed. If the\nL518@P3: tag is found in that wavefront’s portion of the VTA, the LLD sends\nL519@P3: a VTA hit signal to the locality scoring system ( 14 ). These signals\nL520@P3: inform the scoring system that a wavefront has missed on a cache\nL521@P3: line that may have been a hit if that wavefront had more exclusive\nL522@P3: access to the L1D cache.\nL523@P3: 3.3.4. Locality Scoring System Operation Figure 6 provides a\nL524@P3: visual example of the locality scoring system’s operation. In this\nL525@P3: example, there are four wavefronts initially assigned to the com\u0002pute unit. Time T0 corresponds to the time these wavefronts are\nL526@P3: initially assigned to this core. Each segment of the stacked bar rep\u0002resents a score given to each wavefront to quantify the amount of\nL527@P3: intra-wavefront locality it has lost. We call these values lost-locality\nL528@P3: scores (LLS). At T0 we assign each wavefront a constant base lo\u0002cality score. LLS values are stored in a max heap ( 15 ) inside the\nL529@P3: locality scoring system. A wavefront’s LLS can increase when the\nL530@P3: LLD sends a VTA hit signal for this wavefront. The scores each\nL531@P3: decrease by one point every cycle until they reach the base local\u0002ity score. The locality scoring system gives wavefronts losing the\nL543@P3: the score cutoffs every compute unit cycle. In our example from Fig\u0002ure 6, between T0 and T1, W2 has received a VTA hit and its score has\nL544@P3: been increased to the lost-locality detected score (LLDS), ( b in Fig\u0002ure 6). Section 3.3.5 explains the LLDS in more detail. W2’s higher\nL545@P3: score has pushed W3 above the cumulative LLS cutoff, clearing W3’s\nL546@P3: \"Can Issue\" bit if it attempts to issue a load instruction. From a mi\u0002croarchitecture perspective, LLSs are modified by the score update\nL564@P4: above the cutoff as the kernel ends. This results in the system taking\nL565@P4: more time to both constrain multithreading when locality is lost and\nL566@P4: back off thread limiting when there is no lost locality.\nL567@P4: 3.3.5. Determining the Lost-Locality Detected Score (LLDS) The\nL568@P4: value assigned to a wavefront’s score on a VTA hit (the LLDS) is a\nL569@P4: function of the total number of VTA hits across all this compute\nL570@P4: unit’s wavefronts ( 18 ) and all the instructions this compute unit has\nL709@P5: can issue another instruction. This is different from our base\u0002line which prevents a fetched instruction from issuing if the\nL710@P5: scoreboard detects a data hazard.\nL711@P5: Best-SWL Static Wavefront Limiting as described in Section 3.2.\nL712@P5: All possible limitation values (32 to 1) were run and the best\nL713@P5: performing case is picked. The GTO policy is used to select\nL714@P5: between wavefronts. The wavefront value used for each bench\u0002mark is shown in Table 3.\nL715@P5: CCWS Cache-Conscious Wavefront Scheduling described in Sec\u0002tion 3.3 with the configuration parameters listed in Table 3.\nL716@P5: GTO wavefront prioritization logic is used.\nL717@P5: The data for Belady-optimal replacement misses per thousand in\u0002structions (MPKI) presented in Figures 8 and 10 is generated with\nL718@P5: SAGCS:\nL719@P5: <scheduler>-BEL Miss miss rate reported by SAGCS when using\nL720@P5: the Belady-optimal replacement policy. SAGCS is stimulated\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRegular-Dead on Arrival: Characterizing and Protecting Against Dead-Entry TLB Misses in GPU Microarchitectures (https://arxiv.org/html/2606.00486v1)\nciteturn291view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn289view2\",\"pattern\":\"scheduling\"}); Total lines: 374\nL119: TLB-aware scheduling and concurrency. Pichai et al. [cite73†39 ] reduce TLB pressure by grouping cooperative thread arrays with overlapping virtual address footprints onto the same SM, improving L1 TLB locality and lowering the miss rate. MASK [cite47†5 , cite44†56 ] redesigns the GPU memory hierarchy to support multi-application concurrency, addressing TLB shootdown and coherence overheads.\nL120: OASIS [cite74†64 ] introduces object-aware page management for multi-GPU systems, placing memory closer to the GPU that touches it most frequently. Kim et al. [cite75†38 , cite33†61 ] propose batch-aware unified memory management for irregular GPU workloads, coalescing page-management requests to amortize translation overhead. These techniques reduce the frequency of TLB misses through workload-aware scheduling and placement.\nL121: Our characterization focuses on the structural cause of misses that persist regardless of scheduling, specifically the re-walk of recently evicted entries.\nL122: Dead-block and dead-page prediction. The dead-entry concept has a long lineage in cache replacement [cite76†32 , cite77†31 , cite78†33 ]. Khan et al. [cite79†37 ] introduced sampling-based dead-block prediction for last-level caches, identifying blocks unlikely to be reused before eviction and prioritizing them for replacement. Most closely related to our work, Mazumdar et al. [cite80†47 ] propose joint dead-page and dead-block predictors that clean TLBs and caches together using PC-based reuse prediction.\nL123: Our characterization identifies a structurally distinct dead-entry re-walk miss class in GPU L2 TLBs, where eviction history rather than access-PC reuse is the discriminating signal, and quantifies its prevalence across 24 workloads.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMASK: Redesigning the GPU Memory Hierarchyto Support Multi-Application Concurrency (https://rausavar.github.io/pubs/mask-asplos18.pdf)\nciteturn291view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 16; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn287view2\",\"pattern\":\"Determining the Number of Tokens\"}); Total lines: 1794\nL800@P6: 1 TLB-Fill Tokens 2 3 Address-Space-Aware\nL801@P6: Memory Scheduler\nL802@P6: Figure 10. MASK design overview.\nL803@P6: 5.2 Reducing L2 TLB Interference\nL804@P6: Sections 4.1 and 4.2 demonstrate the need to minimize TLB misses,\nL805@P6: which induce long-latency stalls. MASK addresses this need with\nL806@P6: a new mechanism called TLB-Fill Tokens ( 1 in Figure 10). To re\u0002duce inter-address-space interference at the shared L2 TLB, we use\nL807@P6: an epoch- and token-based scheme to limit the number of warps\nL808@P6: from each GPU core that can fill (and therefore contend for) the L2\nL809@P6: TLB. While every warp can probe the shared L2 TLB, only warps\nL810@P6: with tokens can fill the shared L2 TLB. Page table entries (PTEs)\nL811@P6: requested by warps without tokens are only buffered in a small TLB\nL812@P6: bypass cache. This token-based mechanism requires two compo\u0002nents: (1) a component to determine the number of tokens allocated\nL813@P6: to each application, and (2) a component that implements a policy\nL814@P6: for assigning tokens to warps within an application.\nL815@P6: When a TLB request arrives at the L2 TLB controller, the GPU\nL816@P6: probes tags for both the shared L2 TLB and the TLB bypass cache\nL817@P6: in parallel. A hit in either the TLB or the TLB bypass cache yields a\nL818@P6: TLB hit.\nL819@P6: Determining the Number of Tokens. Every epoch,5 MASK\nL820@P6: tracks (1) the L2 TLB miss rate for each application and (2) the\nL821@P6: total number of all warps in each core. After the first epoch,6the\nL822@P6: initial number of tokens for each application is set to a predeter\u0002mined fraction of the total number of warps per application.\nL823@P6: At the end of any subsequent epoch, for each application, MASK\nL824@P6: compares the application’s shared L2 TLB miss rate during the\nL825@P6: current epoch to its miss rate from the previous epoch. If the miss\nL826@P6: rate increases by more than 2%, this indicates that shared TLB\nL827@P6: contention is high at the current token count, so MASK decreases\nL828@P6: the number of tokens allocated to the application. If the miss rate\nL829@P6: decreases by more than 2%, this indicates that shared TLB contention\nL830@P6: is low at the current token count, so MASK increases the number\nL831@P6: of tokens allocated to the application. If the miss rate change is\nL832@P6: within 2%, the TLB contention has not changed significantly, and\nL833@P6: the token count remains unchanged.\nL834@P6: Assigning Tokens to Warps. Empirically, we observe that (1) the\nL835@P6: different warps of an application tend to have similar TLB miss\nL836@P6: rates; and (2) it is beneficial for warps that already have tokens to\nL837@P6: retain them, as it is likely that their TLB entries are already in the\nL838@P6: shared L2 TLB. We leverage these two observations to simplify the\nL839@P6: token assignment logic: our mechanism assigns tokens to warps,\nL840@P6: 5We empirically select an epoch length of 100K cycles.\nL841@P6: 6Note that during the first epoch, MASK does not perform TLB bypassing.\nL842@P6: one token per warp, in an order based on the warp ID (i.e., if there\nL843@P6: are n tokens, the n warps with the lowest warp ID values receive\nL844@P6: tokens). This simple heuristic is effective at reducing TLB thrashing,\nL845@P6: as contention at the shared L2 TLB is reduced based on the number\nL846@P6: of tokens, and highly-used TLB entries that are requested by warps\nL847@P6: without tokens can still fill the TLB bypass cache and thus still take\nL848@P6: advantage of locality.\nL849@P6: TLB Bypass Cache. While TLB-Fill Tokens can reduce thrashing\nL850@P6: in the shared L2 TLB, a handful of highly-reused PTEs may be\nL851@P6: requested by warps with no tokens, which cannot insert the PTEs\nL852@P6: into the shared L2 TLB. To address this, we add a TLB bypass cache,\nL853@P6: which is a small 32-entry fully-associative cache. Only warps with\u0002out tokens can fill the