s have access to the silver queue, each\nL919@P7: application takes turns being assigned to the Silver Queue based\nL920@P7: on two per-application metrics: (1) the number of concurrent page\nL921@P7: walks, and (2) the number of warps stalled per active TLB miss.\nL922@P7: The number of data demand requests each application can add to\nL935@P7: there is no significant performance benefit if the memory controller reorders address\nL936@P7: translation requests within the Golden Queue to exploit row buffer locality.\nL937@P7: To track the number of outstanding concurrent page walks\nL938@P7: (ConPTW in Equation 1), we add a 6-bit counter per application to\nL939@P7: the shared L2 TLB.8 This counter tracks the number of concurrent\nL940@P7: TLB misses. To track the number of warps stalled per active TLB\nL941@P7: miss (W arpsStalled in Equation 1), we add a 6-bit counter to each\nL942@P7: TLB MSHR entry, which tracks the maximum number of warps that\nL943@P7: hit in the entry. The Address-Space-Aware DRAM Scheduler resets\nL944@P7: all of these counters every epoch (see Section 5.2).\nL945@P7: We find that the number of concurrent address translation re\u0002quests that go to each memory channel is small, so our design has\nL946@P7: an additional benefit of lowering the page table walk latency (be\u0002cause it prioritizes address translation requests) while minimizing\nL947@P7: interference.\nL948@P7: 5.5 Page Faults and TLB Shootdowns\nL1129@P9: fairness by allowing both applications to access all shared resources.\nL1130@P9: Second, compared to SharedTLB, which is the baseline that provides\nL1131@P9: the best fairness, MASK reduces unfairness by 22.4% on average.\nL1132@P9: As the number of tokens for each application changes based on the\nL1133@P9: L2 TLB miss rate, applications that benefit more from the shared\nL1134@P9: L2 TLB are more likely to get more tokens, causing applications\nL1135@P9: that do not benefit from shared L2 TLB space to yield that shared\n